 `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING ZERO-MUTEX SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL MUTEX-GUARDED FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package pagination` (the same internal package as the existing `page_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeStore` / `row` / `positionByID` / `rowID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file store, `concurrentPaginationStore`, with a `sync.RWMutex`-guarded rows slice and `insert` / `delete` / `list` / `listAsc` methods — declaring it in the new test file (not in `page_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeStore`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. (2) `WIDEN THE EXISTING FIXTURE WITH AN ASCENDING SIBLING METHOD WHEN THE COVERAGE SCENARIO TABLE NEEDS BOTH DIRECTIONS — KEEP THE DESCENDING-DEFAULT METHOD UNCHANGED` — the existing `fakeStore.list` hard-codes descending order (matches the audit-log / deployment-list canonical case). Rather than refactor it (and risk regressing the existing TestCursor* tests), add a sibling `listAsc` method on the same receiver in the new test file so the closed-set coverage member can iterate both directions through a `storeLike` interface (`list` + `listAsc`) without forking the descending fixture. The interface lives in the new test file so the existing `fakeStore` definition file is untouched. (3) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE COVERS EVERY DISTINCT PAGINATION SHAPE — EMPTY / SINGLE-ROW / EXACT-PAGE / MULTI-PAGE-NO-REMAINDER / MULTI-PAGE-PARTIAL-TRAILING / SMALL-PAGE / BOTH DIRECTIONS` — the scenario-table-rejects-duplicate-names self-check at the head of the coverage member guards against a future addition that quietly drops the closed-set property. Each scenario walks BuildPage end-to-end with a safety-bound terminator (1000-page bound) so the test fails loudly if a regression introduces a non-terminating cursor stream; the diagnostic surfaces both the scenario name AND the page index AND the 